THE MEASUREMENT

verify:navigation measures whether a person can reach a page. Nothing measured
whether the thing that greets them on every page could send them to one. So I
measured the shipped system prompt:

  Floor Studio 0 · camera 0 · /design 0 · /pricing 0 · /quote-check 0 ·
  framework 0 · service-areas 0 · corridors 0 · papers 0 · glossary 0 ·
  samples 0 · photo triage 0        — 6,230 characters, zero paths

And its five tools — get_company_context, estimate_project, get_availability,
book_measure, create_quote_request — are every one of them transactional. NOT
ONE COULD RETURN A PAGE.

So a homeowner asking "can I see what walnut would look like in my living room"
got the six services and an offer of a measure, from a company whose site
renders walnut into a photograph of their living room, live, from their phone
camera, for free, and has since LIVE-01 shipped this morning.

The prompt is not careless. It is 6,230 characters of good instruction about
voice, price honesty, prompt injection, and closing every reply on something
Ecowoods does. It was simply never told what had been built around it. That is
the difference between a booking bot and a front door.

WHAT CLOSES IT

lib/assistant-site.ts, derived from lib/navigation.ts — the same module the
desktop panels, the mobile drawer and ⌘K already read.

  · siteCapabilitiesBlock() is appended to the system prompt at request time.
    packages/shared is upstream of apps/web and cannot import a route table —
    the direction GEO-005 records — so the app appends what it knows rather than
    the prompt hard-coding it.
  · find_on_site, a sixth tool, searches the same destination set and returns
    real paths carrying the menu's own labels and notes. It cannot return a path
    the navigation does not carry, and cannot describe a page differently from
    the panel that links it.
  · The headline features are hrefs plus a TRIGGER — never labels. The words
    come from the navigation, so a page renamed in a menu is renamed in the
    assistant's mouth the same commit.

The live camera leads, and the block carries the honesty with it: it is
rendering, not generation, and that is why the price under it means something.
One recommendation per reply, so a reply stays a reply rather than a directory.

AS-02 · A RECOMMENDATION YOU HAD TO RETYPE

ChatWidget rendered {m.content} as a raw text node. The prompt forbids markdown
for a reason recorded there — a 392px bubble with no renderer turns [a](b) into
literal brackets — and nobody followed it through to the consequence: a path the
assistant named was INERT TEXT. The homeowner was told where to go and then had
to type it themselves.

The widget links the one shape the assistant is told to write, ecowoods.ca/path,
and nothing else. The parsing is a pure function so it can be tested, including
giving a trailing full stop back to the sentence — without that the href is
"/pricing.", a 404, from the most common thing a model writes.

AS-04 · THE TEST THAT EARNED ITS KEEP, BEFORE SHIPPING

The first matcher was /\b(?:https?:\/\/)?(?:www\.)?ecowoods\.ca(\/[…]*)?/g and
its own test — written to assert that no other domain becomes clickable — failed
on ecowoods.ca.evil.example.com/pricing. The href stayed internal, so it was not
an open redirect. What it did was render this company's name as a link in the
middle of somebody else's hostname, inside this company's own chat window. A
trailing (?![A-Za-z0-9\-.]) closes it and the case is asserted by name.

AS-03 · NO SEARCH ON A SMALL PHONE

The ⌘K trigger hides below 379px to leave room for the brand and the hamburger.
A phone has no ⌘. The drawer listed four top links, two accordions, service
areas, a price CTA and login — no search. So on an iPhone SE, 375px, the one
control that reaches all fifty-five destinations on this site had no way in at
all. The drawer has a "Search this site" row now, opening the palette through a
window event rather than a ref threaded through a hamburger. AWS puts search in
the drawer for the same reason.

ALSO RECORDED

verify-pricing-source fired on a price literal in this work's own test file. It
was removed rather than marked pricing-allow, on the same reasoning as the last
time: a band written into a test is a second copy of a published band.

Verified: 66 guards pass. 95 new assertions on the site map, the search and the
link matcher; 65,456 across the floor-studio, navigation and assistant suites,
0 failed. docs/AUDIT_03_INTEGRATION.md carries the working, including what came
back clean — 56 routes within 3 clicks, 643 links landing where they claim,
162/162 images, 36 agentic endpoints, 20 machine editions, and no two pages
doing one job.

The grade is A−, and the missing grade has one name: GC-026. A homeowner in
Amherst reads a United States band on their city page and is one tap from a tool
that prices their floor in Canadian dollars. GEO-006 closes it.

